00:00:24.380 --> 00:00:32.210
So to start with the current pandemic, we've
seen a number of different aspects of the

00:00:32.210 --> 00:00:40.079
global economy exposed by the pandemic, including
the fragility of supply chains, the fragility

00:00:40.079 --> 00:00:46.050
of the sort of social welfare apparatus in
many different countries when employees get

00:00:46.050 --> 00:00:48.410
sick and can't come to work.

00:00:48.410 --> 00:00:52.899
We've seen the world's debt increase significantly.

00:00:52.899 --> 00:00:58.239
When you think about the last two years and
the pandemic's effect on the global economy,

00:00:58.239 --> 00:01:00.500
what are your big concerns and takeaways?

00:01:00.500 --> 00:01:08.639
Well, there are many changes and disasters
at the quantitative level.

00:01:08.639 --> 00:01:11.930
So things that were already bad got much,
much worse.

00:01:11.930 --> 00:01:18.280
So if you were a precariously employed worker,
your life became even more miserable than

00:01:18.280 --> 00:01:24.929
it was within our countries and between our
countries, inequalities have skyrocketed.

00:01:24.929 --> 00:01:27.469
But that's a quantitative effect.

00:01:27.469 --> 00:01:31.039
We had these problems before they have been
magnified.

00:01:31.039 --> 00:01:37.999
If you think about it, the crisis, which begun
in Wall Street in 2008 and caused the Fed

00:01:37.999 --> 00:01:43.409
the European Central Bank, the central banks
of the world, to band together and start printing

00:01:43.409 --> 00:01:49.619
mountain ranges of money in order to support
financial sectors that what happened effectively

00:01:49.619 --> 00:01:53.840
in the first semester of 2009 under Obama.

00:01:53.840 --> 00:02:01.049
Gordon Brown in England and so on was effectively
state's central banks replaced private profit

00:02:01.049 --> 00:02:05.649
in order to flow to the financial sector that
caused huge inequalities.

00:02:05.649 --> 00:02:08.920
Those who were in the money markets never
had so good.

00:02:08.920 --> 00:02:16.469
Those who were not suffered suffered the permanent
stagnation that COVID 19 exacerbated that

00:02:16.469 --> 00:02:18.209
turbocharge that.

00:02:18.209 --> 00:02:24.660
But we are now in the process of another major
shock to the system, which is a qualitative

00:02:24.660 --> 00:02:32.439
shock because between 2009 and 2020, the central
banks have been stabilizing the financial

00:02:32.439 --> 00:02:35.200
markets in a way that I just described.

00:02:35.200 --> 00:02:36.200
Yes.

00:02:36.200 --> 00:02:37.830
And the way that we're all familiar with.

00:02:37.830 --> 00:02:43.129
But now they are between a rock and a hard
place.

00:02:43.129 --> 00:02:44.650
The central banks.

00:02:44.650 --> 00:02:45.650
Why?

00:02:45.650 --> 00:02:46.650
Because.

00:02:46.650 --> 00:02:53.879
They have zombified states and corporations,
so my my country, the government of my country

00:02:53.879 --> 00:02:55.709
is completely zombified.

00:02:55.709 --> 00:03:00.849
It's totally bankrupt and relies on the central
Bank of Europe to keep printing money to keep

00:03:00.849 --> 00:03:03.090
it afloat to a large extent.

00:03:03.090 --> 00:03:08.989
You could even say that to a lesser extent,
but to a large extent about the federal government,

00:03:08.989 --> 00:03:11.150
the United States.

00:03:11.150 --> 00:03:17.989
Your debt relies to a very large extent on
the Fed's capacity and willingness to continue

00:03:17.989 --> 00:03:20.329
to roll it over.

00:03:20.329 --> 00:03:26.060
The same in England, the same thing in Japan
to a large extent.

00:03:26.060 --> 00:03:32.579
But because of the disruption of the supply
chains that you mentioned and the fact that

00:03:32.579 --> 00:03:37.790
we now have serious cost push inflation, cost
push inflation on demand, pull cost, push

00:03:37.790 --> 00:03:44.329
inflation that is putting them in a damned
if they do damned if they don't situation

00:03:44.329 --> 00:03:50.500
central banks, if they continue to refloat
the financial markets, are going to be fueling

00:03:50.500 --> 00:03:55.529
inflation, which is creating significant political
and social problems.

00:03:55.529 --> 00:04:02.599
If they stop doing that, then corporations
and states that rely on this extending and

00:04:02.599 --> 00:04:06.329
pretending of their debt are going to go under.

00:04:06.329 --> 00:04:11.629
And this is going to be the qualitative effect
that we are going to be remembering 10 20

00:04:11.629 --> 00:04:16.310
years time regarding this period, this juncture.

00:04:16.310 --> 00:04:22.460
